---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import StoreButton from "../components/StoreButton.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import { stores } from "../data/stores";
import { isStoreLive, repositoryUrl, securityReportUrl, supportEmail, supportIssuesUrl } from "../site.config";

const storeDomains = stores.map((store) => store.domain);
const supportedStoreList = `${storeDomains.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and ${storeDomains.at(-1)}`;
const securityPolicyUrl = `${repositoryUrl}/security/policy`;
const supportEmailUrl = `mailto:${supportEmail}`;

const sections = {
  "before-you-ask": "Check these first",
  report: "Report a problem",
  "feature-requests": "Suggest an improvement",
  email: "Email the developer",
  security: "Report a security problem",
  "store-listing": "Chrome Web Store",
} as const;

const tocItems = Object.entries(sections).map(([id, heading]) => ({ href: `#${id}`, label: heading }));
---

<Base
  title="Support"
  description="How to get help with Across Amazon, report a problem on GitHub or report a security issue privately."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Support"
    lede="Most questions have a short answer in the FAQ. For anything else, ask the developer on GitHub or by email."
  />
  <div class="container page-body">
    <Toc items={tocItems} />

    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        Your settings and past results stay in your browser. The developer runs no server and receives nothing from the
        extension, so a report has to say what the popup showed.
      </p>
      <p>The source code is <a href={repositoryUrl}>public on GitHub</a> under the MIT licence.</p>

      <h2 id="before-you-ask">{sections["before-you-ask"]}</h2>
      <p>
        These are expected. If one keeps happening on a store where it did not before, <a href="#report">report it</a>.
        The <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> has more on each of them.
      </p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li>
          <h3 id="stores-not-compared">Some stores could not be compared</h3>
          <p>
            They are listed at the bottom of the results, under a line
            such as <span class="nowrap">“4 marketplaces could not be compared”</span>, each with a reason.
            The status <span class="nowrap">“Delivered total not quotable”</span> most often means neither the store
            nor any seller it lists under <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> delivers the item new to
            your country. <span class="nowrap">“Could not set delivery location”</span> means
            the store would not switch to your country, or does not offer it. A store that showed a robot check is listed
            as <span class="nowrap">“Could not read the page”</span>, with a note that says so.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/faq/#stores-that-dont-ship">Why some stores can’t be compared</a></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 id="deliver-to-changed">Another store’s <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box now shows your country</h3>
          <p>
            So that every store quotes delivery to the same place, the extension may set each other store’s delivery
            location to the country in the <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box of the page you started from,
            and leave it that way. Only a country is set, never an address, and the store you started from is not
            changed. To undo it, pick another location in that store’s <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/faq/#delivery-location">Why another store’s <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box shows your country</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 id="check-match">A result is marked <span class="nowrap">“Check match”</span></h3>
          <p>
            The listing was found by searching, not by its ASIN, and its match confidence is below 90%. Open the
            listing and confirm it is the same product, variant and condition before you buy.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/faq/#check-match">What <span class="nowrap">“Check match”</span> means</a></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 id="check-again-instant"><span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> came back at once</h3>
          <p>
            A finished check is reused for 30 minutes. An older result is deleted only when exactly the same check
            runs again; otherwise it stays until you remove the extension or clear its storage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Within 30 minutes of a check, <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> returns the same result from
            the local cache and reads no store again. After that, a fresh check runs. A check in which Amazon showed a
            robot check, or that ran out of time, is not stored, so <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> checks
            every store again.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/faq/#cache">Why <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> can be instant</a></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 id="stay-here">The card reads <span class="nowrap">“Stay here”</span></h3>
          <p>
            No other store beat the delivered total on the page you are on. That page is the highlighted top row, and
            every other row shows how much more it would cost.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/faq/#stay-here">What <span class="nowrap">“Stay here”</span> means</a></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 id="open-a-product-page">The popup asks you to open an Amazon product page</h3>
          <p>
            It shows this, with the check button disabled, on any site other than a supported Amazon store, and on an
            Amazon page that is not a product page, such as search results or the cart.
            It works on product pages on {supportedStoreList}.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/faq/#stores">Which stores it works on</a></p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="report">{sections.report}</h2>
      <p>If the FAQ does not cover it, open an issue on GitHub. Say what you expected and what happened instead.</p>
      <p><a class="button button-primary" href={supportIssuesUrl}>Open an issue on GitHub</a></p>

      <h3 id="what-to-include">What to include</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The store, and the product’s ASIN or the product page address. An address you reached from an Amazon search
          can include your search terms, so you may prefer to give the ASIN.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your destination country as Amazon’s <span class="nowrap">“Deliver to”</span> box shows it, for example
          Switzerland. The country is enough: when you are signed in, that box can also show your first name, town
          and postcode.
        </li>
        <li>
          The exact text the popup showed, such as a store’s status and note
          under <span class="nowrap">“could not be compared”</span>, or the reason on
          a <span class="nowrap">“Couldn’t compare”</span> card. For example, the
          status <span class="nowrap">“Could not set delivery location”</span> with the note “This store does not
          offer delivery to Switzerland.”
        </li>
        <li>Your Chrome version.</li>
        <li>Whether Amazon shows a robot check when you open that store yourself.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="what-never-to-include">What never to include</h3>
      <p>
        Never include passwords, your address, order numbers or screenshots of your account pages. The extension never
        reads your passwords, your orders or your cookies, so a report never needs them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="feature-requests">{sections["feature-requests"]}</h2>
      <p>
        Ideas go to the same place. <a href={supportIssuesUrl}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>, and say what you wanted to
        do and where the extension fell short.
      </p>

      <h2 id="email">{sections.email}</h2>
      <p>
        Questions, problem reports and ideas can also go by email to <a href={supportEmailUrl}>{supportEmail}</a>, for
        example if you have no GitHub account or would rather not post in public. The advice
        on <a href="#what-to-include">what to include</a> and <a href="#what-never-to-include">what never to include</a> also
        applies to email.
      </p>

      <h2 id="security">{sections.security}</h2>
      <p>
        If you find a security problem in the extension or this website, report it privately through GitHub, from the
        repository’s Security tab. Please do not post it as a public issue.
      </p>
      <p>The repository’s <a href={securityPolicyUrl}>security policy</a> explains what to include.</p>
      <p><a class="button button-secondary" href={securityReportUrl}>Report a vulnerability privately</a></p>

      <h2 id="store-listing">{sections["store-listing"]}</h2>
      {
        isStoreLive ? (
          <>
            <p>Install Across Amazon, or read its listing, on the Chrome Web Store.</p>
            <p>
              <StoreButton />
            </p>
          </>
        ) : (
          <p>The Chrome Web Store listing is not published yet.</p>
        )
      }
      <p>
        The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains what the extension reads, what it keeps, where it connects
        and who receives what.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .checks {
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .checks > li {
    padding-block: 20px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .checks > li + li {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .checks > li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .checks h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .checks p + p {
    margin-top: 0.5em;
  }
</style>
